**Read:** [Fluid Mechanics — David Tong](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_3SP698XHQFSZZ6N9Q22NESHMEA), chapter 4 characteristics, wave steepening and shocks; [Computational Fluid Dynamics: An Open-Source Approach — Vermeire and colleagues](../sources/Computational-Fluid-Dynamics-Vermeire-Pereira-Karbasian.lingua#r_7B08DEGENZD4JFFRFNPKJCVFCW), conservative computational treatment; [Notes on Computational Fluid Dynamics — Greenshields and Weller](../sources/Notes-on-CFD-Greenshields-Weller.lingua#r_50W8G5SX00YH7R6TBXK4FAP5MY), advection and conservation-law discretization.

For a scalar conservation law q_t+f(q)_x=0, smooth characteristics move at speed f′(q). If that speed depends on the state, neighboring characteristics can converge and the smooth solution can develop an infinite gradient. Continuing intersecting characteristics as a multivalued physical field is not an admissible continuation.

Integrate the conservation law across a moving discontinuity to obtain the jump speed s=[f]/[q], then impose an entropy condition to select a physical weak solution. For systems, characteristic families, contact discontinuities, shocks and rarefactions interact. A Riemann problem starts from two constant states and provides a local building block for conservative numerical fluxes.

Solve a scalar shock and rarefaction analytically before working a gas-dynamic or shallow-water example. Verify conserved quantities and admissibility rather than comparing only a front's location. Relate the mathematical entropy condition to the physical dissipative limit, and state when the simplified continuum equations omit additional physics within the front.

